messages.content:
Repo law context (abridged, binding): Discord/repo English-only; commit law fixed; never modify the agent-team-ci kit; JS <script> blocks are byte-frozen; scoped CSS only; no new external URLs/dependencies; WCAG AA in both themes; performance and mobile simplicity outrank decoration.

# DESIGN CONSULT — docneural-ui design pass (designer: DeepSeek V4.1-Flash)

You are the lead web designer for **docneural-ui** — a documentation SPA that renders an
org-roam corpus as an interactive neural graph plus a reading surface (SvelteKit, static
deploy, zero runtime deps). Implementation is handled by another engineer; you specify.

## Standing law (Owner, non-negotiable order of priority)

1. **Performance beats eye candy. Always.** Every visual choice must be cheap to paint:
   static composition, borders, spacing, type scale, color discipline. No animation-first
   thinking.
2. **Phones are a primary target.** The phone experience must be: performant, fully
   responsive, lightweight, and SIMPLE. Zero animations on phone. Native document scroll.
   Comfortable 40px+ tap targets. No horizontal overflow at 390px.
3. Identity stays: **dark = night terminal** (near-black blue field, one cyan signal color,
   restrained accents), **light = clean paper** (bright, calm, ink-text). Same structure,
   different material. Cyberpunk mood is carried by typography, tokens and composition —
   NOT by glow, motion, or effects.
4. Accessibility: WCAG AA minimum (4.5:1 body text, 3:1 large text / UI borders) in BOTH
   themes. Status colors must never be the only signal.

## Already removed (do NOT reintroduce)

Scan beams, animated constellation pulses, sheen sweeps, conic ring rotation, membrane
gradients, glitch text, drifting backgrounds, marquee data-bus. The site is static at rest.
Desktop allows at most a few cheap, pausable CSS loops (opacity/transform only) — propose
at most 2 or none.

## What this pass must improve (the actual mandate)

- A coherent **type scale + vertical rhythm** (currently inconsistent across sections).
- **Hero composition** on Home (static): hierarchy, alignment, restraint.
- **Surface design**: cards/panels/borders — layered flat design, consistent radii, hairline
  borders instead of heavy shadows.
- **Color token discipline**: fewer, purposeful tokens; fix ad-hoc hexes; keep the cyan
  signal role; make light theme feel like real paper, not inverted dark.
- **Phone composition**: single column flow, section stacking order, spacing, tap comfort,
  header behavior (sticky header currently uses backdrop-filter — replace with a cheap
  solid translucent background if you judge it expensive).
- Static visual interest: HUD/terminal details done with borders, punctuation, alignment,
  uppercase tracking — zero paint cost.

## Hard constraints

- JS is FROZEN: `<script>` blocks are byte-identical-gated. Never propose script changes.
  Markup edits limited to class hooks and element ordering (no new interactive elements).
- CSS lives in `docneural-ui.css` (global) and `<style>` blocks in the components. Scoped
  only. No new fonts, no external URLs, no images, no new dependencies. Vendored fonts:
  Electrolize (display), JetBrains Mono (mono), Inter (body).
- Do not rename existing classes that scripts toggle (class list you must preserve:
  theme classes, nav/header state classes, graph classes). Additive classes are fine.

## Files provided (same directory)

- `docneural-ui.css` — the global stylesheet (main canvas; ~53KB).
- `home.svelte.txt` — Home route markup + styles (hero, stats, chapters, sources; script removed).
- `app-shell.svelte.txt` — app shell: header/nav, theme toggle, layout chrome (script removed).
- `graph-view.svelte.txt` — graph route shell (canvas view; minimal design surface, script removed).
- `index.html.txt` — document shell (script removed).

## Required output format (exactly these sections)

```
## TOKENS
<complete replacement block(s) for the root/theme custom-property blocks — dark and light>

## PATCH <file> <count>
- ANCHOR: <one exact existing selector line copied from that file>
  ACTION: replace | add-after | remove
  CSS:
    <the exact CSS block>

(repeat per patch; every ANCHOR must be copied verbatim from the provided files)

## PHONE
<the complete revised contents of every <=640px / phone media block you touch>

## RATIONALE
<max 10 bullets, each naming the perf reason>
```

Rules for patches: never paraphrase an ANCHOR; if a selector appears in multiple files,
name the file; keep total output under ~1400 lines; if you would exceed it, prioritize
TOKENS + phone + hero, then list the skipped areas in RATIONALE.


===== FILE: docneural-ui.css =====
/* ==========================================================================
   Docneural UI — theme tokens + Org-exported content styles.

   Name: Docneural UI — doc + neural: the reading surface of a documentation
   graph. Hebb's law says neurons that fire together wire together — every
   read of an interlinked doc strengthens the connection in a human brain,
   and every citation resolved strengthens an LLM's context.

   LOOK: a HUD, not a magazine. Ink-blue black, neon cyan/magenta/electric
   yellow, monospace chrome in uppercase with wide tracking, sharp corners
   with one notched edge, corner brackets instead of soft shadows, a faint
   circuit grid and scanlines on the page. Long-form prose stays quiet and
   readable (Inter, no glow) — the neon belongs to the instrument panel, not
   to the text you are trying to read.

   MULTI-SOURCE: this site hosts documentation from many projects (Org, Emacs,
   Guix, ...). Each source owns one neon hue, and the hue is not a second
   palette: it is the SAME colour the graph canvas paints that source's nodes
   with, because core/source.ts hashes the source tag with orui's djb2 and
   indexes the SAME --dn-graph-palette the canvas reads. Components receive it
   as an inline `--dn-src` custom property and use `var(--dn-src)`.

   Structure:
   1. Tokens (dark default + light override) — every color/size the theme
      uses lives here, so downstream sites can re-skin with one block.
   2. Base/reset — page chrome: circuit grid, scanlines, links, caret.
   2b. HUD vocabulary — notches, brackets, glitch, chips, source tint.
   2c. Machine output — key/value grids, selection.
   2d. Chrome — header, nav, status rows, section headers, footer.
   3. `.dn-org` — styling for raw org-export HTML (headings, tables, src
      blocks, TODO badges…). The site injects unmodified Org HTML here; the
      theme must NOT depend on any JS.
   ========================================================================== */

/* ---------- 0. Fonts ---------------------------------------------------
   Self-hosted, latin subset, three files (96 kB total, cached forever).
   WHY: a render-blocking <link> to fonts.googleapis.com delays
   DOMContentLoaded by however long that round trip takes — on the machine
   this site is built on it added tens of seconds to first paint, and on a
   phone it is a DNS + TLS + CSS fetch before the font files even start.
   `font-display: swap` means text paints in the fallback immediately and
   the real face swaps in; nothing waits on a network.
   Inter and JetBrains Mono ship as variable files, so one URL serves both
   weights we use (same arrangement Google's own CSS uses). */
@font-face {
  font-family: "Electrolize";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("../fonts/electrolize-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "JetBrains Mono";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("../fonts/jetbrains-mono-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "JetBrains Mono";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 600;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("../fonts/jetbrains-mono-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Inter";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("../fonts/inter-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Inter";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 600;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("../fonts/inter-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
}

/* ---------- 1. Tokens -------------------------------------------------- */
:root {
  /* Type. Mono IS the identity: headings, nav, labels, meta, code. Human
     prose stays on a quiet sans, because a long read in mono is not a long
     read — Inter carries the body text and nothing else. */
  --dn-font-mono: "JetBrains Mono", "Iosevka", "IBM Plex Mono", ui-monospace,
    "DejaVu Sans Mono", Menlo, monospace;
  --dn-font-prose: "Inter", ui-sans-serif, system-ui, "Segoe UI", Roboto,
    sans-serif;
  /* The brand voice, used ONLY where a shape is more useful than a word:
     the wordmark and the reader's page title. Electrolize is single-weight
     and cramped small, so nothing readable is set in it — one file, 17 kB. */
  --dn-font-display: "Electrolize", "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
  /* Tracking for the uppercase micro-labels that make this read as an
     instrument panel. The ramp: tight for code-ish tags, base for labels,
     wide for overline kickers and the status bar. */
  --dn-track: 0.16em;
  --dn-track-tight: 0.08em;
  --dn-track-wide: 0.28em;

  /* Type-size ramp (rem). Only what the chrome + prose actually use; the
     prose section sizes off these so heading rhythm is one scale. */
  --dn-text-xs: 0.7rem;   /* micro-labels, chips */
  --dn-text-sm: 0.85rem;  /* table headers, code wells */
  --dn-text-md: 1rem;     /* body */
  --dn-text-lg: 1.12rem;  /* h3 */
  --dn-text-xl: 1.3rem;   /* h2 */
  --dn-text-2xl: 1.7rem;  /* h1 / page title */

  /* Spacing ramp — 4px base unit, 8 steps. Chrome rhythm (nav gaps, panel
     padding, section margins) reads off this so everything lands on the
     same grid the circuit pattern draws. */
  --dn-space-1: 0.25rem;
  --dn-space-2: 0.5rem;
  --dn-space-3: 0.75rem;
  --dn-space-4: 1rem;
  --dn-space-5: 1.5rem;
  --dn-space-6: 2rem;
  --dn-space-7: 3rem;
  --dn-space-8: 4rem;

  /* Radii — the HUD is sharp; radii exist only to take the pixel-sting off
     filled elements (chips, wells). Never above 4px, never on panels. */
  --dn-radius-sm: 2px;
  --dn-radius: 3px;
  --dn-radius-lg: 4px;

  /* Surfaces — VOID black (designer round-3 spec: "deeper pit, so emissive
     elements breathe"), keeping the SIGNAL pass's raised text tiers so the
     contrast complaint stays answered. Cool, never neutral grey: the cast
     is what sells the night-city look. */
  --dn-bg: #030608;
  --dn-bg-raise: #0c1219; /* panels, table stripes, code wells */
  --dn-bg-sunken: #05090d; /* page backdrop behind the raise panels */
  --dn-fg: #eaf3fc;
  /* Secondary text and hairlines have to survive a dim room on a cheap
     laptop panel. Tuned for contrast, not subtlety (r3 spec §5, lifted by
     the SIGNAL pass): body ~16:1, muted ~9:1, faint ~5.5:1, borders 3:1. */
  --dn-fg-muted: #a9bfd6;
  --dn-fg-faint: #8ba3bd;
  --dn-border: #416487;
  --dn-border-strong: #5d89b4;

  /* Signals — the cyberpunk set (designer r3): cyan is the wire, hot pink
     the synapse, yellow the alert, acid the LIVE readout, violet the brand
     gradient. Hues at SIGNAL-pass brightness. */
  --dn-accent: #00f0ff;   /* primary: links, focus, active chrome */
  --dn-accent-dim: #2cc4e3; /* accent, safe as text on ink */
  --dn-accent-2: #ff3366; /* secondary: tags, counts, the firing set */
  --dn-accent-2-text: #ff3366;
  --dn-hot: #ffdf3f;      /* attention: hover, backlink arrows, reticle */
  --dn-amber: #ffb347;    /* TODO markers, warnings */
  --dn-acid: #39ff14;     /* live/online readouts: LED, uplink kicker */
  --dn-violet: #bd00ff;   /* gradient borders + the wordmark's middle stop */

  /* The wordmark: a neon-sign decay from electric cyan through violet into
     hot pink. One recipe, shared by the header brand and the hero. */
  --dn-wordmark: linear-gradient(135deg,
    #7df9ff 0%, var(--dn-accent) 26%, var(--dn-violet) 62%, var(--dn-accent-2) 100%);

  /* One shared glow recipe, so "neon" is a constant and not 40 hand-tuned
     box-shadows. R3: hotter — the bloom is the cyberpunk tell. */
  --dn-glow: 0 0 7px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 70%, transparent),
    0 0 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 30%, transparent);
  --dn-glow-2: 0 0 7px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 70%, transparent),
    0 0 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 30%, transparent);
  /* The acid readout gets its own small halo (status LEDs, LIVE kickers). */
  --dn-glow-acid: 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-acid) 75%, transparent),
    0 0 18px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-acid) 35%, transparent);

  /* Corner-bracket recipe, as tokens: stroke width, arm length, and the
     neon bleed under the strokes. .dn-hud and .dn-brackets both read these,
     so "a bracket" is a constant across pseudo-element and gradient
     implementations. */
  --dn-bracket-w: 2px;
  --dn-bracket-l: 14px;
  --dn-bracket-bleed: drop-shadow(0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 60%, transparent));

  /* Graph wires, as a LITERAL color: the force-graph canvas cannot read
     custom properties or parse color-mix(), so the embed bridges
     --orui-link-color to this resolved value (DocneuralGraph). The engine's
     LOD tiers multiply this per zoom band (neuronEngine.ts), so the token is
     the NEAR-band color: at corpus zoom wires drop far below this, which is
     how the cyan burn-out died. 0.28 keeps the mini-map (no LOD there)
     readable while the canvas tiers go quieter at distance. */
  --dn-graph-link: rgba(0, 240, 255, 0.28);

  /* The canvas backdrop: one step lighter than the page, so the graph reads
     as a lit panel sunk into the HUD. GraphView paints a transparent canvas,
     so this DOM color shows through it (DocneuralGraph .roam-wrap). */
  --dn-graph-bg: #0a1524;

  /* The graph's grid: fine circuit lines rather than petri-dish dots. These
     are the colors the grid's linear-gradients paint with; both themes carry
     the same two tokens so the well reads as one material everywhere. */
  --dn-dish-dot-1: rgba(0, 240, 255, 0.1);
  --dn-dish-dot-2: rgba(255, 51, 102, 0.06);

  /* Node colors, as a LITERAL comma list: orui's canvas reads
     --orui-node-palette off the container (see DocneuralGraph/SynapseRail) and
     picks hue = hash(first tag) % length. core/source.ts hashes the SAME way
     and indexes THIS SAME list, so a source's DOM colour and its graph colour
     are one value, not two that happen to look alike. R3 palette: the
     designer's cyberpunk hues at SIGNAL-pass brightness. */
  --dn-graph-palette: #00f0ff, #ff3366, #ffdf3f, #bd00ff, #39ff14, #ff7a45, #56c8ff, #ff5c8a;

  /* Geometry — sharp. The one notch is the signature; --dn-notch feeds the
     clip-path shapes in §2b. */
  --dn-notch: 9px;
  --dn-max-prose: 74ch;
  --dn-header-h: 3.2rem;
  --dn-side-w: 240px;
  --dn-panel-w: 340px;

  color-scheme: dark;
}

/* Day mode: same network, bleached to paper. Opt in with
   <html data-theme="light">; the JS theme toggle only flips this attribute.
   The neon darkens into ink-jet colour so the HUD survives a bright room.
   SIGNAL pass: this is a first-class look now, not an afterthought — real
   paper (brighter floor), real ink (darker text), saturated signal colours,
   and every token re-checked against WCAG AA (text >= 4.5:1, borders >= 3:1).
   Full parity: the glow recipes and bracket bleed are re-tuned for paper
   too, because a dark-theme bloom on white reads as a dirty smudge. */
:root[data-theme='light'] {
  --dn-bg: #f4f7fb;
  --dn-bg-raise: #ffffff;
  --dn-bg-sunken: #e5ecf4;
  --dn-fg: #0b1524;
  --dn-fg-muted: #38506a;
  --dn-fg-faint: #556a80;
  --dn-border: #66839c;
  --dn-border-strong: #47647f;
  --dn-accent: #00688f;
  --dn-accent-dim: #056a8a;
  --dn-accent-2: #c91375;
  --dn-accent-2-text: #a80e5f;
  --dn-hot: #7a5c00;
  --dn-amber: #8f5600;
  /* Ink-jet versions of the two new signals: readable as text on paper. */
  --dn-acid: #0b7a12;
  --dn-violet: #8a00d1;
  /* The wordmark keeps its decay on paper with the darkened stops. */
  --dn-wordmark: linear-gradient(135deg,
    #056a8a 0%, var(--dn-accent) 30%, var(--dn-violet) 66%, var(--dn-accent-2) 100%);
  /* Glows on paper: shorter throw, tighter core — a printed halo, not a
     lamp. Keeps focus rings and hover states legible in a bright room. */
  --dn-glow: 0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 45%, transparent),
    0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 18%, transparent);
  --dn-glow-2: 0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 45%, transparent),
    0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 18%, transparent);
  --dn-glow-acid: 0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-acid) 50%, transparent),
    0 0 10px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-acid) 22%, transparent);
  --dn-bracket-bleed: drop-shadow(0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 35%, transparent));
  --dn-graph-link: rgba(0, 104, 143, 0.5);
  --dn-graph-bg: #ffffff;
  --dn-dish-dot-1: rgba(0, 104, 143, 0.16);
  --dn-dish-dot-2: rgba(201, 19, 117, 0.1);
  --dn-graph-palette: #00688f, #c91375, #7a5c00, #5b3fd6, #007a5c, #c2410c, #0369a1, #b3125f;
  color-scheme: light;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
  /* Honor the OS unless the page explicitly asked for dark. Kept in sync with
     the block above by hand — there is no build step, so a token added to one
     must be added to the other. */
  :root:not([data-theme]) {
    --dn-bg: #f4f7fb;
    --dn-bg-raise: #ffffff;
    --dn-bg-sunken: #e5ecf4;
    --dn-fg: #0b1524;
    --dn-fg-muted: #38506a;
    --dn-fg-faint: #556a80;
    --dn-border: #66839c;
    --dn-border-strong: #47647f;
    --dn-accent: #00688f;
    --dn-accent-dim: #056a8a;
    --dn-accent-2: #c91375;
    --dn-accent-2-text: #a80e5f;
    --dn-hot: #7a5c00;
    --dn-amber: #8f5600;
    --dn-acid: #0b7a12;
    --dn-violet: #8a00d1;
    --dn-wordmark: linear-gradient(135deg,
      #056a8a 0%, var(--dn-accent) 30%, var(--dn-violet) 66%, var(--dn-accent-2) 100%);
    --dn-glow: 0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 45%, transparent),
      0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 18%, transparent);
    --dn-glow-2: 0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 45%, transparent),
      0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 18%, transparent);
    --dn-glow-acid: 0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-acid) 50%, transparent),
      0 0 10px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-acid) 22%, transparent);
    --dn-bracket-bleed: drop-shadow(0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 35%, transparent));
    --dn-graph-link: rgba(0, 104, 143, 0.5);
    --dn-graph-bg: #ffffff;
    --dn-dish-dot-1: rgba(0, 104, 143, 0.16);
    --dn-dish-dot-2: rgba(201, 19, 117, 0.1);
    --dn-graph-palette: #00688f, #c91375, #7a5c00, #5b3fd6, #007a5c, #c2410c, #0369a1, #b3125f;
    color-scheme: light;
  }
}

/* Phones (and any chrome-under-a-notch context): the header height grows by
   the top safe-area inset so EVERY surface's `calc(100dvh - header)` math
   stays true (synapse canvas, reader, home). The header itself pads by the
   same inset (DocneuralApp); the graph chips pad their bottom edge the same
   way. On devices without a notch env() is 0 and nothing moves. */
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  :root {
    --dn-header-h: calc(3.2rem + env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px));
  }
}

/* ---------- 2. Base ----------------------------------------------------- */
*,
*::before,
*::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

body.dn-body {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--dn-font-prose);
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: 1.65;
  color: var(--dn-fg);
  /* The circuit board the HUD sits on: one fine grid, one coarse grid. Two
     sizes so the page has depth without a heavy pattern, and both are hairline
     1px lines — SIGNAL pass sizing (72/14px), alphas up a step. R3 adds the
     layer the designer called the vignette mask: a fixed radial that pulls
     the page's edges into the void, so the center of every surface reads
     closer to the eye than its frame. background-attachment is per-layer;
     only the vignette is fixed, so the grid scrolls and the void does not. */
  background-color: var(--dn-bg);
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(ellipse at 50% 42%, transparent 52%, color-mix(in srgb, black 62%, transparent) 100%),
    linear-gradient(to right, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 5.5%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 5.5%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to right, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 3.5%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 3.5%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px);
  background-size: 100% 100%, 72px 72px, 72px 72px, 14px 14px, 14px 14px;
  background-attachment: fixed, scroll, scroll, scroll, scroll;
}
:root[data-theme='light'] body.dn-body {
  /* On paper the void is a soft grey bloom, not black. */
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(ellipse at 50% 42%, transparent 55%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 70%, transparent) 100%),
    linear-gradient(to right, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 5.5%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 5.5%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to right, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 3.5%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 3.5%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px);
}

/* The aurora: four slow, pre-blurred signal glows pinned off the corners of
   the viewport (two bright on the leading diagonal, two faint framing the
   other diagonal), behind all content (z-index -1) — the board's power rails
   leaking light at all four corners, not just two. The gradients ARE soft,
   so there is no filter() blur cost; only transform/opacity animate, and the
   layer is fixed, so it composites once and never repaints on scroll.
   prefers-reduced-motion parks it. */
body.dn-body::before {
  content: "";
  position: fixed;
  inset: -14%;
  z-index: -1;
  pointer-events: none;
  background:
    radial-gradient(44% 36% at 16% 6%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 14%, transparent), transparent 70%),
    radial-gradient(40% 32% at 88% 94%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 12%, transparent), transparent 70%),
    radial-gradient(34% 28% at 86% 8%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 6%, transparent), transparent 70%),
    radial-gradient(34% 28% at 12% 92%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 6%, transparent), transparent 70%);
  animation: dn-aurora 26s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
}
@keyframes dn-aurora {
  from { transform: translate3d(-2.2%, -1.4%, 0) scale(1); opacity: 0.8; }
  to { transform: translate3d(2.2%, 1.4%, 0) scale(1.07); opacity: 1; }
}
:root[data-theme='light'] body.dn-body::before {
  /* On paper the aurora is a wash of tint, not a lamp: keep it faint. */
  opacity: 0.5;
  animation-name: dn-aurora-soft;
}
@keyframes dn-aurora-soft {
  from { transform: translate3d(-2.2%, -1.4%, 0) scale(1); }
  to { transform: translate3d(2.2%, 1.4%, 0) scale(1.07); }
}

/* Scanlines: the CRT overlay every cyberpunk HUD has. Fixed and full-bleed so
   it survives scrolling, pointer-events:none so it never eats a click, and
   above the canvas too — a screen effect that stops at the graph would look
   like a bug. Kept at 3% so it reads on a bright monitor and does not turn
   the text into a strobe. */
body.dn-body::after {
  content: "";
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: 60;
  pointer-events: none;
  background: repeating-linear-gradient(
    to bottom,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 55%, transparent) 0 1px,
    transparent 1px 3px
  );
  opacity: 0.45;
  mix-blend-mode: multiply;
  /* Corner presence: a radial mask lets the scanlines breathe over the
     reading area and deepen toward the edges/corners, so the screen reads as
     a lit CRT face — brighter at the center of attention, denser where the
     tube curves away. A static mask composites once; nothing repaints. */
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(130% 130% at 50% 44%, transparent 46%, black 100%);
  mask-image: radial-gradient(130% 130% at 50% 44%, transparent 46%, black 100%);
}

/* The scan SWEEP was considered here (designer effect #3) and dropped in the
   R3 merge: the SIGNAL aurora already owns body::before, and aurora +
   scanlines + vignette + brackets is the right effect budget — a third
   full-viewport animated layer bought nothing but frame risk. The retrace
   idea survives in the scanline mask deepening toward the tube's edge. */
:root[data-theme='light'] body.dn-body::after {
  /* On paper the overlay has to be light-on-dark or it just greys the page. */
  mix-blend-mode: screen;
  background: repeating-linear-gradient(
    to bottom,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 80%, transparent) 0 1px,
    transparent 1px 3px
  );
}

.dn-body a {
  color: var(--dn-accent);
  text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 45%, transparent);
  text-underline-offset: 3px;
  transition: color 120ms, text-shadow 120ms;
}
.dn-body a:hover {
  color: var(--dn-hot);
  text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-hot) 65%, transparent);
}
:root[data-theme='light'] .dn-body a:hover {
  /* On paper the hot-yellow halo reads as a stain; a firmer underline does
     the hovering instead. */
  text-shadow: none;
  text-decoration-color: var(--dn-hot);
}

:where(a, button, input, [tabindex]):focus-visible {
  outline: 1px solid var(--dn-accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
  box-shadow: var(--dn-glow);
}

.dn-mono {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
}

/* Motion vocabulary. `.spark` (the ⌁ glyph) breathes; content rises in on
   mount; a landed anchor flashes so a stub→section redirect is visible.
   All opt out under prefers-reduced-motion (block at the end of § 2b). */
@keyframes dn-pulse {
  0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
  50% { opacity: 0.35; }
}
@keyframes dn-rise {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(6px); }
  to { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}
@keyframes dn-flash {
  from { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 30%, transparent); }
  to { background: transparent; }
}
/* The status LED's beat: scale + opacity only (compositor-friendly), twice
   as fast as the old opacity fade — a heartbeat, not a breathing exercise. */
@keyframes dn-led {
  0%, 100% { transform: scale(1); opacity: 1; }
  50% { transform: scale(0.78); opacity: 0.6; }
}
/* Terminal cursor blink for the hero feed line (Home). */
@keyframes dn-blink {
  0%, 49% { opacity: 1; }
  50%, 100% { opacity: 0; }
}

/* Thin themed scrollbars keep the rails feeling like one instrument. */
* {
  scrollbar-width: thin;
  scrollbar-color: var(--dn-border-strong) transparent;
}
*::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 9px; height: 9px; }
*::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
  background: var(--dn-border-strong);
  border-radius: 0;
  border: 2px solid transparent;
  background-clip: content-box;
}
*::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover { background: var(--dn-accent-dim); }

/* ---------- 2b. HUD vocabulary -----------------------------------------
   The DOM-side equivalents of what the canvas paints. Everything here is
   sharp-edged and lit from the edge: brackets, notches, underline-glow —
   no soft 20px-radius cards anywhere.                                       */

/* The brand mark: a node in cross-section. Kept as a ring + nucleus so it
   reads at 0.7em in the header and at 3em in the hero. The membrane pulses:
   the mark is never static, even when it means "idle". */
.dn-neuron-mark {
  display: inline-grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 1.1em;
  height: 1.1em;
  border-radius: 50%;
  color: var(--dn-accent);
  border: 1px solid currentColor;
  background: radial-gradient(circle,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 45%, transparent) 0,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 6%, transparent) 62%,
    transparent 100%);
  box-shadow: 0 0 10px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 38%, transparent);
  /* PERF-MOBILE: the membrane pulse is gone — animating box-shadow repaints
     every frame, on every surface the mark sits on (brand, stats head,
     controls). The static glow IS the look. */
}
.dn-neuron-mark::before {
  content: "";
  width: 0.34em;
  height: 0.34em;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 92%, white);
  box-shadow: 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 80%, transparent);
}

/* Firing indication for anything "synapse" (counts, tabs, chips): the
   accent breathes, matching the canvas particles' direction of travel. */
.dn-firing {
  color: var(--dn-accent);
  animation: dn-pulse 2.4s ease-in-out infinite;
}

/* The graph well: the canvas sits inside this. A circuit grid (finer than the
   page's, and brighter — this is the lit panel) plus an inward vignette, so
   the neurons read as live signal on a board rather than dots on a flat pane.
   Class name kept as .dn-dish because both graph mounts already use it. */
.dn-dish {
  background-color: var(--dn-graph-bg);
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(to right, var(--dn-dish-dot-1) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--dn-dish-dot-1) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to right, var(--dn-dish-dot-2) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--dn-dish-dot-2) 1px, transparent 1px),
    radial-gradient(ellipse at center,
      transparent 55%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 60%, transparent) 100%);
  background-size: 32px 32px, 32px 32px, 8px 8px, 8px 8px, 100% 100%;
}

/* Floating surfaces — the command palette, the note preview, the graph
   controls. SIGNAL pass: sharper glass — a darker fill behind a neon-tinted
   1px edge and a wider shadow, so a panel reads as a slab of black glass
   laid over the board. Still no backdrop-filter: on a 4 GB machine a big
   backdrop-filter costs frames, and the border does the glowing anyway. */
.dn-glass {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-raise) 92%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 70%, transparent));
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 32%, var(--dn-border-strong));
  box-shadow:
    inset 0 1px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 18%, transparent),
    0 14px 40px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 85%, transparent),
    0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 6%, transparent);
}

/* Corner brackets: the signature. Two pseudo-elements draw an L at opposite
   corners, so any region reads as a framed HUD target — the trick that
   replaces a soft drop shadow. SIGNAL pass took the strokes to 2px/12px;
   R3 extends the arms to 14px and adds the designer's neon bleed under the
   strokes. Needs position:relative on the host, and the host must not clip
   its own overflow. Stroke/arms/bleed all come from the §1 bracket tokens. */
.dn-hud {
  position: relative;
}
.dn-hud::before,
.dn-hud::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  width: var(--dn-bracket-l);
  height: var(--dn-bracket-l);
  pointer-events: none;
  color: var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent));
  opacity: 0.9;
  filter: var(--dn-bracket-bleed);
}
.dn-hud::before {
  top: -1px;
  left: -1px;
  border-top: var(--dn-bracket-w) solid currentColor;
  border-left: var(--dn-bracket-w) solid currentColor;
}
.dn-hud::after {
  right: -1px;
  bottom: -1px;
  border-right: var(--dn-bracket-w) solid currentColor;
  border-bottom: var(--dn-bracket-w) solid currentColor;
}

/* Four-corner brackets as a pure background — for hosts that already spend
   both pseudo-elements (or clip their overflow and can't use .dn-hud). Eight
   gradient strokes, one per corner-arm, drawn from the same tokens. No
   pseudo-elements, no extra markup; composites once and never repaints. */
.dn-brackets {
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)), var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent))),
    linear-gradient(var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)), var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent))),
    linear-gradient(var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)), var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent))),
    linear-gradient(var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)), var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent))),
    linear-gradient(var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)), var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent))),
    linear-gradient(var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)), var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent))),
    linear-gradient(var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)), var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent))),
    linear-gradient(var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)), var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)));
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
  background-size:
    var(--dn-bracket-l) var(--dn-bracket-w), var(--dn-bracket-w) var(--dn-bracket-l),
    var(--dn-bracket-l) var(--dn-bracket-w), var(--dn-bracket-w) var(--dn-bracket-l),
    var(--dn-bracket-l) var(--dn-bracket-w), var(--dn-bracket-w) var(--dn-bracket-l),
    var(--dn-bracket-l) var(--dn-bracket-w), var(--dn-bracket-w) var(--dn-bracket-l);
  background-position:
    0 0, 0 0,
    100% 0, 100% 0,
    0 100%, 0 100%,
    100% 100%, 100% 100%;
}

/* The notched edge: one corner cut at 45°, the silhouette of a hardware
   label. Used on filled chrome (chips, CTAs, segmented buttons) where there
   is no border to clip. clip-path also clips box-shadow, so anything that
   needs an OUTER glow wants .dn-hud instead of a notch. */
.dn-notch {
  clip-path: polygon(
    0 0,
    calc(100% - var(--dn-notch)) 0,
    100% var(--dn-notch),
    100% 100%,
    var(--dn-notch) 100%,
    0 calc(100% - var(--dn-notch))
  );
}
/* The half-notch: only the leading corner cut — for elements already in a
   row (tabs, breadcrumb segments) where two cuts fight the neighbours. */
.dn-notch-lead {
  clip-path: polygon(
    0 0,
    calc(100% - var(--dn-notch)) 0,
    100% var(--dn-notch),
    100% 100%,
    0 100%
  );
}

/* Uppercase micro-label — the small caps of the HUD. Anything that is a
   caption rather than content goes through this so "SYSTEM // NODES" and a
   sentence never look like the same kind of text. */
.dn-label {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: var(--dn-text-xs);
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--dn-fg-muted);
}
/* The kicker: the widest-tracking tier, for the one line above a page or
   panel title ("CORPUS // GNU EMACS"). Wider than a label, dimmer than a
   heading — the hierarchy is track width, not size. */
.dn-kicker {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: var(--dn-text-xs);
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track-wide);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--dn-fg-faint);
}

/* Chip: the tag/source pill. Square-ish, 1px edge, colour from --dn-src when
   the host set one (see the MULTI-SOURCE note above). SIGNAL pass: the edge
   and the fill both rose so a chip reads from across the page. */
.dn-chip {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: 0.72rem;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  color: var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent-2));
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent-2)) 12%, transparent);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent-2)) 45%, transparent);
  border-radius: var(--dn-radius-sm);
  padding: 0.12em 0.6em;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
/* The lit chip: for the one active filter/tab in a row of chips. Same hue,
   but the fill doubles and the edge goes solid, so "selected" is weight,
   not a second colour. */
.dn-chip.active {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent-2)) 24%, transparent);
  border-color: var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent-2));
}

/* The lit edge: a bar that grows out of an element when it becomes active —
   the DOM echo of an axon leaving a soma. Row-like things (cards, chapters,
   palette entries) all share it, so "the lit edge" means one thing everywhere.
   Same idea as .dn-spine from the previous pass, kept under that class name so
   existing markup keeps working, now without the round glow. */
.dn-spine { position: relative; }
.dn-spine::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  top: 50%;
  width: 2px;
  height: 0;
  background: var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent));
  box-shadow: 0 0 8px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 70%, transparent);
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  transition: height 160ms ease;
  pointer-events: none;
}
.dn-spine.active::before,
.dn-spine:hover::before { height: 72%; }

/* Glitch wordmark — the hero's whole reason to look like a screen. The text
   is real (::before/::after carry the SAME string from data-text, so the
   glyphs stay selectable and the accessible name is untouched), and the two
   offset copies are cyan and magenta: the chromatic split of a bad signal.
   It fires briefly on a 6s loop rather than strobing, so it reads as an
   event, not a headache. */
.dn-glitch {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
}
/* PERF-MOBILE: the ghosts park at opacity 0 — same treatment reduced-motion
   already gave them, because "a frozen chromatic split looks like misrendered
   text" (this file's own words). The wordmark keeps its chromatic fringe via
   the text-shadow under the gradient; the 6s steps() loops are gone from
   every page load. */
.dn-glitch::before,
.dn-glitch::after {
  content: attr(data-text);
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  opacity: 0;
  clip-path: inset(0 0 60% 0);
}
.dn-glitch::before {
  color: var(--dn-accent);
}
.dn-glitch::after {
  color: var(--dn-accent-2-text);
  clip-path: inset(55% 0 0 0);
}

/* Brand gradient text — the wordmark and section headers. R2: the gradient
   IS the neon-sign decay (cyan -> violet -> hot pink, the designer's token),
   and the aberration shadows ride underneath it: text-shadow paints the
   glyph silhouette even when the fill is transparent, so the fringe reads
   while the gradient carries the face. Kept as a background-clip trick so it
   survives every theme (both stops are live tokens, not baked hues). */
.dn-gradient-text {
  background: var(--dn-wordmark, linear-gradient(100deg,
    var(--dn-accent), var(--dn-violet), var(--dn-accent-2)));
  background-size: 220% 100%;
  -webkit-background-clip: text;
  background-clip: text;
  color: transparent;
  text-shadow:
    -2px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 55%, transparent),
    2px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 45%, transparent),
    0 0 30px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 30%, transparent);
  /* PERF-MOBILE: the 9s background-position sweep is parked — a gradient
     that never stops repainting the wordmark bought little; the decay
     gradient at rest is the look. */
  background-position: 0% 0;
}
:root[data-theme='light'] .dn-gradient-text {
  /* On paper the aberration fringe is noise, not neon: drop the offsets and
     keep only the faintest cool halo so the wordmark stays ink-crisp. */
  text-shadow: 0 0 18px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 18%, transparent);
}

/* Hairline rule that reads as a trace on the board: a 1px line with a lit
   node riding on it. Used between hero blocks and above footers. */
.dn-trace {
  position: relative;
  height: 1px;
  border: 0;
  margin: 1.6rem 0;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg,
    transparent, var(--dn-accent-dim) 10%, var(--dn-border-strong) 50%, var(--dn-accent-dim) 90%, transparent);
}
.dn-trace::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  width: 5px;
  height: 5px;
  margin: -2.5px 0 0 -2.5px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--dn-accent);
  box-shadow: 0 0 12px 2px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 65%, transparent);
  /* PERF-MOBILE: the lit node rests mid-trace instead of drifting forever. */
  transform: translateX(0);
}

/* A halo behind a hero/panel: two stacked rings, so a DOM surface reads as
   live signal next to the graph. */
.dn-halo {
  position: relative;
}
.dn-halo::before,
.dn-halo::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  border-radius: 50%;
  border: 1px solid var(--dn-accent);
  pointer-events: none;
}
.dn-halo::before { inset: -0.35em; opacity: 0.4; }
.dn-halo::after { inset: -0.7em; opacity: 0.18; }

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .spark { animation: none; }
  .dn-firing { animation: none; }
  body.dn-body::before { animation: none; opacity: 0; }
  .dn-gradient-text { animation: none; }
  .dn-trace::after { animation: none; }
  .dn-neuron-mark { animation: none; }
  .dn-led-dot { animation: none; }
  /* The glitch is the one effect that cannot merely stop: a frozen chromatic
     split looks like misrendered text, so the ghosts go entirely. */
  .dn-glitch::before,
  .dn-glitch::after { animation: none; opacity: 0; }
  * { transition-duration: 0.01ms !important; animation-duration: 0.01ms !important; }
}

/* ---------- 2c. Machine output ------------------------------------------
   Numbers the site computed (corpus stats, counts) are set in mono and put
   in a key/value grid. That is the whole vocabulary now: an earlier revision
   dressed this content as a shell session — a tty window with title-bar
   glyphs, a PS1 prompt, `[ ok ]` lines, a blinking block cursor — and it made
   a documentation site look like it was mid-boot. The terminal feel stays in
   the DESIGN (monospace, tracking, notched corners, the neon trio), not in
   props that pretend the page is a terminal.                                   */

/* A column of keys, a column of values. Grid rather than a table because it
   is not tabular data. */
.dn-kv {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
  column-gap: 1.4em;
  row-gap: var(--dn-space-1);
}
.dn-kv > span:nth-child(odd) { color: var(--dn-fg-faint); }
.dn-kv > span { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.dn-kv b { color: var(--dn-hot); font-weight: 600; }

/* Text you select should read as part of the HUD: a solid neon block,
   not the browser's blue. */
::selection {
  background: var(--dn-accent);
  color: var(--dn-bg-sunken);
}

/* ---------- 2d. Chrome --------------------------------------------------
   Header, nav, status rows, section headers, footer. The hierarchy rule:
   track width and border weight do the ranking, not size jumps — an
   instrument panel reads top-to-bottom as kicker > label > value.         */

/* The header bar: a glass strip with a neon-tinted bottom hairline, the
   board's top edge. Height comes from --dn-header-h so every
   `calc(100dvh - var(--dn-header-h))` stays honest. */
.dn-header {
  min-height: var(--dn-header-h);
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--dn-space-4);
  padding: 0 var(--dn-space-4);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-raise) 82%, transparent);
  border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 28%, var(--dn-border));
  box-shadow: 0 1px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 10%, transparent);
}
.dn-header .dn-label { color: var(--dn-fg-faint); }

/* Primary nav: mono, uppercase, base tracking. The active link is lit by an
   underline trace in the source hue — no pills, no background fill, so the
   row keeps one rhythm and "where you are" is a wire, not a badge. */
.dn-nav {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--dn-space-5);
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: var(--dn-text-sm);
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track);
  text-transform: uppercase;
}
.dn-nav a {
  color: var(--dn-fg-muted);
  text-decoration: none;
  padding-bottom: 2px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
  transition: color 120ms, border-color 120ms;
}
.dn-nav a:hover {
  color: var(--dn-fg);
  text-shadow: none;
  border-bottom-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 55%, transparent);
}
.dn-nav a.active,
.dn-nav a[aria-current] {
  color: var(--dn-accent);
  border-bottom-color: var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent));
}

/* A status row: the LED-and-readout strip ("LIVE · 1,386 NODES · SYNCED").
   Widest tracking tier; the LED beats in acid. */
.dn-status {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--dn-space-3);
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: var(--dn-text-xs);
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track-wide);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--dn-fg-faint);
}
.dn-status b { color: var(--dn-fg-muted); font-weight: 600; }
.dn-led-dot {
  width: 6px;
  height: 6px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--dn-acid);
  box-shadow: var(--dn-glow-acid);
  animation: dn-led 1.6s ease-in-out infinite;
}

/* Section header: kicker over a mono title over a trace. The bus-line under
   it carries the source hue, matching the h1 rule in §3, so "a new section"
   looks the same in chrome and in prose. */
.dn-section-head {
  margin: var(--dn-space-6) 0 var(--dn-space-4);
  padding-bottom: var(--dn-space-2);
  border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 45%, var(--dn-border));
}
.dn-section-head .dn-kicker { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--dn-space-1); }
.dn-section-head h2 {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: var(--dn-text-xl);
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track-tight);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--dn-fg);
}

/* The footer: quiet ink, one trace on top, mono meta row. It closes the
   instrument — no glow, nothing firing. */
.dn-footer {
  margin-top: var(--dn-space-7);
  padding: var(--dn-space-4);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--dn-border);
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: var(--dn-text-xs);
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track-tight);
  color: var(--dn-fg-faint);
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: var(--dn-space-2) var(--dn-space-5);
}
.dn-footer a { color: var(--dn-fg-muted); }

/* ---------- 3. Org content (.dn-org) -----------------------------------
   org-export produces plain semantic HTML (div.outline-N, h1..h4, table,
   pre.src, span.todo/markup). Style it here; components mount content
   inside a container with class="dn-org".

   Prose rules for this section: the BODY TEXT stays a quiet sans with no
   glow — a HUD that makes you work to read a manual is a failed HUD. The
   neon belongs to structure: headings, table headers, code wells.         */

.dn-org {
  max-width: var(--dn-max-prose);
  animation: dn-rise 220ms ease-out both;
}

/* Paragraph rhythm: no indent, one blank line's worth of air. Text is the
   quiet tier — nothing here glows, ever. */
.dn-org p {
  margin: 0 0 1em;
  orphans: 3;
  widows: 3;
}
.dn-org p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

/* When the reader lands on a heading (TOC click, prev/next, or a stub
   redirect into a chapter), flash it so the eye finds the spot. Org-export
   gives every headline a real id, so :target fires on the URL fragment. */
.dn-org [id]:target,
.dn-org :target {
  animation: dn-flash 1.4s ease-out 1;
  scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--dn-header-h) + 12px);
}

/* JS-driven sibling of the above: the reader scrolls with scrollIntoView (no
   URL fragment), so it adds .dn-flash to the landed heading and removes it
   when the animation ends. */
.dn-flash {
  animation: dn-flash 1.4s ease-out 1;
  scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--dn-header-h) + 12px);
}

.dn-org h1, .dn-org h2, .dn-org h3, .dn-org h4,
.dn-org h5, .dn-org h6 {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.25;
  letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  margin: 1.8em 0 0.6em;
  scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--dn-header-h) + 12px);
}
/* The first heading of a chapter doesn't need the top margin — the outline
   wrapper already supplies the air. */
.dn-org h1:first-child, .dn-org h2:first-child, .dn-org h3:first-child {
  margin-top: 0;
}

.dn-org h1 {
  font-size: var(--dn-text-2xl);
  color: var(--dn-fg);
  /* The page title is the one place a glow earns its keep: it is a header,
     not a paragraph, and it marks the top of the doc. Under it, a neon rule
     in the source's hue — the chapter's bus line. */
  text-shadow: 0 0 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 30%, transparent);
  border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 55%, var(--dn-border-strong));
  box-shadow: 0 1px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 16%, transparent);
  padding-bottom: 0.35em;
}
:root[data-theme='light'] .dn-org h1 {
  /* On paper the title glow is a halo smudge; the bus line alone carries it. */
  text-shadow: none;
}
/* h2/h3 carry a mono sigil in the source's hue (set --dn-src by the reader):
   a heading level is legible from the margin before you read a word. */
.dn-org h2 {
  font-size: var(--dn-text-xl);
  letter-spacing: 0.005em;
  padding-bottom: 0.2em;
  /* Overline rule in the section's own hue — quiet (mostly border ink) but
     never anonymous: every section sits on its corpus's bus line. */
  border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 34%, var(--dn-border));
}
.dn-org h2::before {
  content: "// ";
  color: var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent));
  text-shadow: 0 0 10px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 45%, transparent);
}
:root[data-theme='light'] .dn-org h2::before { text-shadow: none; }
.dn-org h3 { font-size: var(--dn-text-lg); }
.dn-org h3::before {
  content: "» ";
  color: var(--dn-src, var(--dn-border-strong));
}
.dn-org h4 { font-size: var(--dn-text-md); color: var(--dn-fg-muted); }
/* Deep levels go small-caps rather than smaller-and-smaller: at h5 the
   hierarchy flips to weight+tracking, which keeps the scale from shrinking
   into unreadability. */
.dn-org h5 {
  font-size: var(--dn-text-sm);
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track-tight);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--dn-fg-muted);
}
.dn-org h6 {
  font-size: var(--dn-text-sm);
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track-tight);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--dn-fg-faint);
}

/* A heading with an id is a link target: clicking it copies a deep link to
   that section (DocReader labels them and handles the click). The # only
   appears on hover/focus so the margin stays quiet while you read. */
.dn-org h2[id], .dn-org h3[id] {
  cursor: pointer;
  /* Keep the same header offset as every other target — an earlier revision
     set this to 0.6rem and landed headings slid under the fixed header. */
  scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--dn-header-h) + 12px);
}
.dn-org h2[id]::after, .dn-org h3[id]::after {
  content: " #";
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  color: var(--dn-accent);
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 120ms linear;
}
.dn-org h2[id]:hover::after, .dn-org h3[id]:hover::after,
.dn-org h2[id]:focus-visible::after, .dn-org h3[id]:focus-visible::after {
  opacity: 1;
}

/* Org TODO keywords on headlines: org exports <span class="todo">TODO</span>
   and <span class="done">DONE</span> — note .done does NOT also carry .todo,
   so the base badge styles must cover both (an earlier revision only styled
   .todo and DONE rendered as bare amber text). Both are filled badges with a
   single notched corner: status you can read from the margin, mid-scroll. */
.dn-org .todo, .dn-org .done {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-weight: 600;
  font-size: 0.74em;
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  padding: 0.15em 0.55em;
  border: 1px solid;
  border-radius: var(--dn-radius-sm);
  margin-right: 0.6em;
  white-space: nowrap;
  vertical-align: 0.08em;
  clip-path: polygon(
    0 0,
    calc(100% - 4px) 0,
    100% 4px,
    100% 100%,
    0 100%
  );
}
.dn-org .todo {
  border-color: var(--dn-amber);
  color: var(--dn-amber);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-amber) 10%, transparent);
}
.dn-org .done {
  border-color: var(--dn-accent-dim);
  color: var(--dn-accent-dim);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-dim) 10%, transparent);
}

/* Tags as #chips — the same chip the rest of the HUD uses, because a tag
   means one thing in the article and one thing in the sidebar. */
.dn-org .tag {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: 0.72em;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 8%, transparent);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 30%, transparent);
  border-radius: var(--dn-radius-sm);
  padding: 0.1em 0.55em;
  margin-left: 0.35em;
  color: var(--dn-accent-2-text);
  white-space: nowrap;
}

.dn-org p code,
.dn-org li code,
.dn-org td code {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: 0.88em;
  background: var(--dn-bg-raise);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 26%, var(--dn-border));
  border-radius: var(--dn-radius-sm);
  padding: 0.08em 0.4em;
  color: var(--dn-accent);
}
/* Code inside a pre is not a chip — strip the inline treatment so the well
   owns the styling. */
.dn-org pre code {
  background: none;
  border: 0;
  padding: 0;
  color: inherit;
}

/* Source blocks: a well sunk into the page, with a NEON left rail (the
   source's hue, glowing softly) so it reads as machine output at a glance
   even mid-scroll. */
.dn-org pre {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: var(--dn-text-sm);
  line-height: 1.5;
  tab-size: 4;
  background: var(--dn-bg-sunken);
  border: 1px solid var(--dn-border);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent));
  border-radius: var(--dn-radius-sm);
  padding: 0.9em 1.1em;
  margin: 1.2em 0;
  overflow-x: auto;
  box-shadow:
    -3px 0 10px -4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 55%, transparent),
    inset 0 0 24px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 60%, transparent);
  transition: border-color 160ms ease, box-shadow 160ms ease;
}
.dn-org pre:hover {
  border-left-color: var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent));
  box-shadow:
    -3px 0 14px -3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 70%, transparent),
    inset 0 0 24px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 60%, transparent);
}
.dn-org pre.example {
  border-left-color: var(--dn-accent-2);
  box-shadow:
    -3px 0 10px -4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 55%, transparent),
    inset 0 0 24px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 60%, transparent);
}
:root[data-theme='light'] .dn-org pre,
:root[data-theme='light'] .dn-org pre.example {
  /* On paper the rail's glow becomes a clean edge; the well shades instead. */
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 18px color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-sunken) 55%, transparent);
}

/* Org tables: borderless grids with hairlines; header row is a column of
   uppercase mono under a NEON rule — the instrument look for data. */
.dn-org table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
  margin: 1.2em 0;
  font-size: 0.92rem;
  max-width: 100%;
}
.dn-org th, .dn-org td {
  padding: 0.35em 0.9em 0.35em 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--dn-border);
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
}
.dn-org th {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-weight: 600;
  font-size: 0.85em;
  letter-spacing: var(--dn-track-tight);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--dn-accent);
  border-bottom: 2px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-src, var(--dn-accent)) 60%, var(--dn-border-strong));
}
/* Zebra: every second body row sits on the raised surface — the stripe
   reads as a panel seam, not a fill, so the data stays quiet. */
.dn-org tbody tr:nth-child(even) td {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-bg-raise) 55%, transparent);
}
.dn-org tr:hover td {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent) 6%, transparent);
}

.dn-org blockquote {
  margin: 1.2em 0;
  padding: 0.4em 1.2em;
  border-left: 3px solid var(--dn-accent-2);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dn-accent-2) 6%, transparent);
  color: var(--dn-fg-muted);
  font-style: italic;
}

.dn-org ul, .dn-org ol { padding-left: 1.4em; }
.dn-org li { margin: 0.25em 0; }
.dn-org li::marker { color: var(--dn-border-strong); }

/* Org footnotes and targets */
.dn-org .footref a, .dn-org .target { scroll-margin-top: 100px; }
.dn-org .footref { font-size: 0.8em; vertical-align: super; }
.dn-org .footdefined, .dn-org .figure { margin: 1.4em 0; }
.dn-org .caption {
  font-family: var(--dn-font-mono);
  font-size: 0.82rem;
  color: var(--dn-fg-muted);
  text-align: center;
}

.dn-org img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }

.dn-org hr {
  border: none;
  height: 1px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, var(--dn-border-strong), transparent);
  margin: 2em 0;
}

/* Org exports the document title and property drawers as spans; drawers
   stay hidden, the title takes the display face — the one place in the
   reader where the brand voice is allowed. */
.dn-org .title { font-family: var(--dn-font-mono); }

/* Graph hues remain on edges and markers; readable labels use verified
   foreground colors so every source stays AA-safe in both themes. */
.dn-body .src-name,
.dn-body .src-open,
.dn-body .mk-src,
.dn-body .chapter.active,
.dn-body .pager-btn .pt,
.dn-body .src-chip {
  color: var(--dn-accent);
}
.dn-body .seg.active { color: var(--dn-accent); }

/* Keep interactive chrome comfortable on touch screens without stretching
   prose links or dense data rows. */
:where(.dn-body .seg, .dn-body .tool, .dn-body .menu, .dn-body .cpy,
  .dn-body .raw, .dn-body .pager-btn, .dn-body .resume, .dn-body .src-card,
  .dn-body .cta, .dn-body .card, .dn-body .mk, .dn-body .tab,
  .dn-body .tag-chip, .dn-body .link-btn, .dn-body .more, .dn-body .expand,
  .dn-body .chapter, .dn-body .home, .dn-body .pinned button) {
  min-height: 2.5rem;
}

@media (max-width: 700px) {
  .dn-org { line-height: 1.72; }
  .dn-org h1 { font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 7vw, var(--dn-text-2xl)); }
  .dn-org h2 { font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 5.5vw, var(--dn-text-xl)); }
}

/* ---------- PERF-MOBILE (≤700px): the static phone board -----------------
   Designer: glm-5v-turbo (design-forge crown, FINAL 9.0/9). Owner law:
   performance beats eye candy; phones get "performance, responsiveness,
   lightweight and simplicity". Desktop keeps its five cheap survivors
   (aurora, shell rail, power rail, caret, spark); the phone keeps none of
   them — every surface rests on its composed frame. Last in the cascade so
   the parks outrank every base rule above. The body's fixed attachment also
   drops on phones: a background-attachment: fixed layer forces repaints
   into every scroll frame on mobile GPUs (and iOS ignores it anyway), so
   the vignette rides the grid like the rest. */
@media (max-width: 700px) {
  body.dn-body::before { animation: none; }
  .spark, .dn-firing, .dn-led-dot { animation: none; }
  body.dn-body { background-attachment: scroll, scroll, scroll, scroll, scroll; }
}


